   DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  llllvvmm--ccoovv ggccoovv tool reads code coverage data files and displays the
       coverage information for a specified source file. It is compatible with
       the  ggccoovv  tool from version 4.2 of GGCCCC and may also be compatible with
       some later versions of ggccoovv.

       To use llllvvmm--ccoovv ggccoovv, you must first build an instrumented  version  of
       your  application  that collects coverage data as it runs. Compile with
       the --ffpprrooffiillee--aarrccss and --fftteesstt--ccoovveerraaggee options to add the  instrumenta‐
       tion. (Alternatively, you can use the ----ccoovveerraaggee option, which includes
       both of those other options.)

       At the time you compile the instrumented code, a ..ggccnnoo data  file  will
       be  generated  for  each object file. These ..ggccnnoo files contain half of
       the coverage data. The other half of the data comes  from  ..ggccddaa  files
       that  are generated when you run the instrumented program, with a sepa‐
       rate ..ggccddaa file for each object file. Each time you  run  the  program,
       the  execution  counts  are summed into any existing ..ggccddaa files, so be
       sure to remove any old files if you do not want their  contents  to  be
       included.

       By  default, the ..ggccddaa files are written into the same directory as the
       object files, but you can override that by setting the GGCCOOVV__PPRREEFFIIXX  and
       GGCCOOVV__PPRREEFFIIXX__SSTTRRIIPP environment variables. The GGCCOOVV__PPRREEFFIIXX__SSTTRRIIPP variable
       specifies a number of directory components to be removed from the start
       of  the  absolute  path  to  the object file directory. After stripping
       those directories, the prefix from the GGCCOOVV__PPRREEFFIIXX variable  is  added.
       These  environment  variables allow you to run the instrumented program
       on a machine where the original object file directories are not  acces‐
       sible,  but  you will then need to copy the ..ggccddaa files back to the ob‐
       ject file directories where llllvvmm--ccoovv ggccoovv expects to find them.

       Once you have generated the coverage data files, run llllvvmm--ccoovv ggccoovv  for
       each  main  source file where you want to examine the coverage results.
       This should be run from the same directory where you previously ran the
       compiler.  The  results  for the specified source file are written to a
       file named by appending a ..ggccoovv suffix. A separate output file is  also
       created  for  each  file  included by the main source file, also with a
       ..ggccoovv suffix added.

       The basic content of an ..ggccoovv output file is a copy of the source  file
       with  an  execution  count and line number prepended to every line. The
       execution count is shown as -- if a line does not contain any executable
       code.  If  a  line  contains code but that code was never executed, the
       count is displayed as ##########.

